3. User Stories (MVP)

Authentication

As a first-time user, I want to sign up easily so I can start tracking my bills. (Firebase Auth)

As a returning user, I want to log in quickly so I can access my subscription list.

Subscription Management

As a user, I want to add a new subscription by providing its name, monthly cost, and next renewal date so the app can track it.

As a user, I want to see a list of all my active subscriptions, sorted by the soonest renewal date, so I know what is coming up.

As a user, I want to see the total sum of my monthly and yearly subscription costs on the dashboard so I can visualize my spending.

Core Alert Feature

As a user, when I save a subscription, I want the app to schedule a notification for 7 days before the renewal date.

As a user, I want to receive a push notification that says "Cancel or Keep? [Subscription Name] renews in 7 days for $[Cost]" so I have time to act.

As a user, when I tap the notification, I want to be taken directly to the subscription list in the app.

Monetization Enforcement

As a free user, when I try to add my 6th subscription, I want to see a dialog explaining the 5-subscription limit and an option to upgrade.

4. Technical and Implementation Details

4.1 Frontend (Android Native)

Language: Kotlin

UI Framework: Jetpack Compose (Focus on Material 3 Design for a clean, fast UI).

Architecture: Simple MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) pattern to separate UI logic from business logic.

UI Components: Use Scaffold, LazyColumn, and Card components for the main list. Use a DatePickerDialog for inputting the renewal date.

4.2 Backend and Data Persistence

Service: Google Firebase (Spark Plan - Free Tier).

Database: Cloud Firestore for storing subscription data.

Structure: /subscriptions/{subscriptionId}

Fields per Document: name: String, cost: Double, renewalDate: Timestamp, userId: String, isPro: Boolean (default: false).

4.3 Notifications (The Highest Priority)

Mechanism: Android Local Notifications and AlarmManager.

Rationale: Since the alerts are tied to manually entered dates and do not require real-time server interaction (like stock market changes), local scheduling is more reliable, simpler, and completely free.

Implementation Flow:

User saves a subscription with renewalDate.

The ViewModel/Repository calculates alertTime = renewalDate - 7 days.

A unique ID is generated for the subscription.

The Android AlarmManager is used to set a precise, one-time alarm for the alertTime.

The alarm triggers a BroadcastReceiver, which then creates and displays the notification using NotificationManagerCompat.

4.4 Freemium Gate Logic

The SubscriptionRepository must include a function to count active subscriptions for the current userId.

The main "Add Subscription" action handler will call this counter. If count >= 5 and the user is not marked as isPro, the application will display the upgrade dialog instead of the input form.

5. Future Scope (Phase 2)

Features to be considered after the MVP is stable and validated.

In-App Purchase Integration: Implement Stripe or Google Play Billing for the Premium Tier purchase (unlimited subscriptions, category linking).

Categorization: Allow users to tag subscriptions (e.g., Streaming, Utilities, SaaS) and filter the list by category.

Statistics: Implement a chart/graph view of spending trends (e.g., total cost over the last 6 months).

Cloud Function Backend (for advanced notifications): If the local notification model proves unreliable for some users, migrate the scheduling logic to a Firebase Cloud Function that sends notifications via Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM). This is slightly more complex but ensures server-side reliability.

Currency Support: Allow users to select their local currency and display costs accordingly.

Theming: Add a dark mode option.
